{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>I have not eaten of it in my mourning</b> (בְאֹנִי). Even though the declaration concerns all the tithes, as in the midrashic exposition — 'and I also gave it to the Levite and to the stranger, to the orphan and to the widow' (Sifrei 13, Ma'aser Sheni 3:10) — nevertheless, <b>I have not eaten of it in my mourning</b> (בְאֹנִי) refers back to consecrated produce alone, which is second tithe and fourth-year planting (נטע רבעי), for these are forbidden to an onen (a mourner before burial) and in impurity:" "<b>'And I have not given of it to the dead'</b> — to make for him a coffin and shrouds; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 26:14). But I do not understand this, for it is explicitly stated in the verse (above, 14:25–26) that second tithe may not be redeemed outside Jerusalem except for stamped silver, and in Jerusalem one gives the silver for something fit for eating — in cattle, sheep, wine, and strong drink — and eats it before YHWH. And the Rav, Rabbi Moses, wrote in his composition (Mishneh Torah, Hilkhot Ma'aser Sheni 3:9) that second tithe is given for eating and drinking, as it is written, 'and you shall eat there before YHWH your Elohim,' and anointing is like drinking, and it is forbidden to spend it on other needs, such as taking with it vessels, garments, or slaves, as it is written, 'and I have not given of it to the dead' — meaning, I did not spend it on something that does not sustain the body. Thus the word <b>למת</b> ('to the dead') here is a euphemism for things by which a person does not live, and these are words of Derash. And others explain that he declares he did not give of it for a coffin and shrouds even for a dead person, which is a mitzvah, and all the more so for a living person for a cloak or a tunic. And in the Sifrei (Tavo 14): 'And I have not given of it to the dead' — to make for him a coffin and shrouds; these are the words of Rabbi Eliezer. Rabbi Akiva said to him: if for the dead it is forbidden, then for the living it is also forbidden; so what does 'I have not given of it' teach? That I did not exchange it even for something pure. The interpretation of Rabbi Akiva expounds 'and I have not given of it' to mean: I did not make from it exchanges even for something pure that is fit for eating, in the manner that we learned (Ma'aser Sheni 1:1): one may not sell it and one may not exchange it, and a person may not say to his fellow in Jerusalem, 'Here is wine for you, and give me oil.' And <b>למת</b> ('to the dead'), according to his view, connects to what precedes it: 'I have not eaten of it in my mourning (בְאֹנִי) for a dead person' — for the state of onen applies only to a dead person. And I saw in the Yerushalmi (Ma'aser Sheni 2:1): Rabbi Eliezer said in the name of Rabbi Simai, 'I have not given of it to the dead' — what case are we dealing with? If it is to bring a coffin and shrouds, that is something forbidden for a living person, so for the living it is forbidden, all the more so for the dead. What then is something permitted for the living but forbidden for the dead? That is anointing. I do not understand this either, for if one anointed a dead person with oil of tithe, behold he has burned it in impurity, and he has already declared concerning it, <b>'I have not burned of it in impurity'</b> (לֹא בִעַרְתִּי מִמֶּנּוּ בְּטָמֵא). And in the Gemara in the chapter Ha'arel (Yevamot 70a), Rabbi Shimon ben Lakish said in the name of Rabbi Simai: From where do we derive that second tithe that became impure is permitted for anointing? For it is written, 'and I have not given of it to the dead' — to the dead I did not give it, but for a living person who is like a dead person I did give it. What is something that applies equally to the living and the dead? You must say: anointing. And there they raised the objection: but perhaps he took with it a coffin and shrouds? And the meaning is that it would be permitted for a living person to take with it a tunic, once it became impure, since 'and you shall eat there before YHWH your Elohim' no longer applies to it. And they resolved: <b>מִמֶּנּוּ</b> — from its very body. It remains now that the declaration in <b>'I have not given of it to the dead'</b>, joined with <b>'I have not burned of it in impurity'</b>, constitutes a declaration that he did not eat of it in impurity and did not give of the impure produce to a dead person to anoint him. But in the Mishnah (Ma'aser Sheni 5:12) we learned according to the words of Rabbi Eliezer: 'I have not given of it to the dead' — I did not take with it a coffin and shrouds for a dead person; I did not give it to other onenim (mourners). And the implication is that they expound it to mean he did not give it to the dead — neither for the body of the dead person himself, for a coffin and shrouds, nor to his onenim — so that he is declaring about his own state of onenut and that of others. And because of the onenut of others, he would declare about the coffin and shrouds even though it is forbidden even for a living person."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {1 "The Yerushalmi passage attributed to 'Rabbi Eliezer in the name of Rabbi Simai' appears to conflate two distinct Tannaitic positions; Ramban himself flags his difficulty with it in the immediately following sentence."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:15:48.662966407Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}